[- Concept: Identity, canonical form. -]
[page id='concept-identity'
  [meta
    title='Identity and Canonical Form'
    layout='concept'
    nav-order='220'
    audience='learner'
    summary='Hash the canonical bytes. Declared ids and references on top.'
  ]

  [section id='two-layers' title='Two Layers of Identity'
    [prose Identity in CX has two layers. The first is content-addressed — every tree has a SHA-256 over its canonical bytes; two trees with the same data have the same hash. The second is declarational — an element can carry an id via the hash sigil, and other places in the document can reference it via the at-sigil.]
  ]

  [section id='canonical' title='Canonical Form'
    [prose The canonical mode strips presentation — comments, anchors, whitespace, attribute order normalisation — and emits a data-equivalent byte-identical form. Two documents with the same data emit to the same bytes; the difference between them is something a human added, not something the data carries.]
    [code lang='shell' src='''cx canonical menu.cx     # canonical bytes
cx hash menu.cx          # SHA-256 hex over canonical bytes
cx eq a.cx b.cx          # exit 0 iff canonical(a) == canonical(b)''']
    [prose Canonical form is the basis for content-addressed identity (cx hash), for byte-identical conformance tests across bindings, and for diff that ignores presentation. If two trees should be considered equal, canonical is what compares them.]
  ]

  [section id='declared' title='Declared Identifiers'
    [prose Declared identity is what you write when you want one element to point at another by name. Declare with the hash sigil; reference with the at-sigil. IDs are unique within their scope; the scope is the document root by default.]
    [code lang='cx' src='''[order #main
  [item #latte]
  [item #scone]]
[receipt order=@main]''']
  ]

  [section id='anchors' title='Anchors and Merge Targets'
    [prose An anchor (ampersand sigil) names a value once and lets later places refer to it without repeating it. A merge target (asterisk sigil) folds the anchor referent into the current element. Anchors are anonymous handles; the canonical form renumbers them so two documents that bind the same data with different anchor names hash to the same identity.]
    [code lang='cx' src='''[defaults &shared timeout=30 retries=3]
[server *shared name=api]
[server *shared name=worker]''']
  ]

  [section id='ast-bin' title='ast-bin and CXDB'
    [prose ast-bin is the binary serialization of the canonical form. CXDB is the on-disk byte-stable archive of a CX document. Both round-trip byte-for-byte with the text form; both are versioned via capability bits on the C ABI.]
  ]

  [section id='related' title='Related'
    [list
      [item path='comparison' label='Format conversion — what canonical preserves and what it normalises']
      [item path='comparison'        label='Identity guarantees vs other formats']
    ]
  ]

  [preserve id='author-notes'
    [- Hand-written notes survive regeneration. Empty by default. -]
  ]
]
